[7] SMS Assist

[8] SMS Assist© is a text messaging service for people who are deaf, hard of hearing or speech impaired, which can be used for contacting the WA Police Force and requesting assistance. It should not be used for emergencies.

[9] To use the service we strongly urge you to [register your details first](mailto:PAC@police.wa.gov.au?subject=SMS%20Assist%20%E2%80%93%20Registration%20Request&body=Good%20afternoon%2C%0A%0AThank%20you%20for%20your%20enquiry%20regarding%20registration%20for%20WA%20Police%E2%80%99s%20SMS%20Assist%20Service%20for%20people%20with%20hearing%20or%20speech%20impairments%20to%20contact%20WA%20Police%20and%20request%20assistance.%0A%0AIf%20you%20have%20a%20communication%20disability%20and%20would%20like%20to%20register%20for%20this%20service%2C%20please%20provide%20the%20following%20information%20to%20complete%20your%20registration%3A-%0A%0ADisability%20type%3A%0AGiven%20Name%3A%0ASurname%3A%0ADate%20of%20Birth%3A%0AMobile%20number%3A%0AEmail%20Address%3A%0AAddress%3A%0ASuburb%3A%0APostcode%3A%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%0ADo%20you%20have%20a%20Next%20of%20Kin%20or%20Person%20to%20Contact%3F%20%20If%20so%20please%20provide%20%3A-%0A%0AGiven%20Name%20%3A%0ASurname%20%3A%0ADate%20of%20Birth%20%3A%0AContact%20Number%201%20%3A%0AContact%20Number%202%20%3A%0AAddress%20%3A%0ASuburb%20%3A%0APostcode%20%3A%0A%20%0AOnce%20registered%20you%20will%20be%20able%20to%20contact%2Frequest%20Police%20Assistance%20via%20SMS%20assist%20on%200403%20277%20478.%0A%0AKind%20regards%2C%0A%0APolice%20Assistance%20Centre%20%7C%20Communications%20Division%20%7C%20WA%20Police%0ASMSAssist%C2%A9%3A%200403%20277%20478%0A). Once registered, dial 0403 277 478.

[10] Using SMS Assist©

[11] People who are deaf, hard of hearing or unable to speak clearly can use SMS Assist© to contact the police for:

[12] - requesting police attendance

[13] - reporting matters to police

[14] - A police operator will then send a reply.

[15] It is designed and exclusive for people with communication disabilities (deaf, hard of hearing or speech impaired) only. This applies even if your disability is temporary or only occurs at certain times e.g. when you become stressed.

[16] To help police answer quickly, it is important that you provide the following information:

[17] - where you are messaging from (eg, street name, popular place, church, library, museum, shop name, etc). This way, police know where you are and can send help quickly if it is required.

[18] - where any incident has taken place. This is only needed if the place is different from where you are messaging.

[19] - why you need police help (give a few details of what has happened).

[20] - your name - if you are not registered. Enter text as follows:

[21] - type "MN" (My Name) to save typing "my name is"

[22] - type your family name in uppercase

[23] - think about saving this as a message template.

[24] Example:

[25] A man stole a handbag& ran 2wards train stn

[26] Example:

[27] On Murray St Mall

[28] Example:

[29] I am at Ntrance 2 Aust Post in Forrest Pl Perth

[30] As you can see from the text count, it is possible to put a lot of important information within the 160-character limit. The sample message above used only 109 letters and spaces.

[31] Note: the success of this service depends on the accuracy and details of the information you provide. Please follow the above instructions carefully, especially in relation to giving your exact location.

[32] Contact us on 131 444 for more information.

